---
import { readFileSync } from "node:fs";
import { resolve } from "node:path";
import WeekPost from "@/layouts/WeekPost.astro";
import { ACTIVE_YEAR } from "@/config";
import CodeSnippet from "@/components/CodeSnippet.astro";
import Disclosure from "@/components/workshop/Disclosure.astro";
import Connection from "@/components/llm-playground/Connection.astro";
import Lesson from "@/components/llm-playground/Lesson.astro";
import { outcomesIntroHTML } from "../../../../public/llm-playground/visual-outcomes/ui.js";
import { lessons } from "../../../../public/llm-playground/catalog.js";
import "../../../../public/llm-playground/workshop.css";
import "../../../../public/llm-playground/visual-outcomes/style.css";
import Outcome from "@/components/llm-playground/Outcome.astro";
import { outcomes } from "../../../../public/llm-playground/visual-outcomes/catalog.js";
import { resourcesHTML } from "../../../../public/llm-playground/resources.js";
import banner from "@/assets/workshop-ntoo-installation.jpg";
const shared = readFileSync(resolve("public/llm-playground/shared.js"), "utf8");
---

<WeekPost
  title="Workshop 4: Local Chain of Thoughts"
  description="Read a small example, predict what will happen, then test it with your local model."
  week={13}
  year={ACTIVE_YEAR}
  contentClass="playground-content"
  heroImage={banner}
  heroImageAlt="Stephanie Dinkins’s Not the Only One, a voice-interactive sculpture with faces on a rounded vessel, displayed on a wooden pedestal."
  heroImageCaption={"Stephanie Dinkins, [Not the Only One (N’TOO)](https://www.stephaniedinkins.com/ntoo.html), ongoing since 2018. A voice-interactive AI sculpture built from three generations of family oral histories and hosted on local computers. Installation photograph via [DOTDOT](https://dotdot.studio/project/not-the-only-one/)."}
  pageLinks={[
    { href: "/week-13/", label: "Back to Week 13" },
    { href: "/week-12/llama/", label: "Local Model APIs" },
  ]}
>
  <ul class="workshop-intro-list">
    <li>
      <strong>Start with the instructions you send.</strong> These workshops let
      you examine what happens between typing into a language model and getting a
      response. We’ll begin with prompts, then try examples that search for sources,
      use a calculator, revise a floor plan, or carry a summary into the next conversation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Change something and compare the result.</strong> Each example gives
      you something specific to edit and a response you can inspect. Before running
      it, predict what will happen, then compare that with what the program actually
      does.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Work here or open the files yourself.</strong> Open Llama App or Ollama,
      load a chat model, and connect below. The examples send requests to the model
      on your own computer. You can also <a
        href="/llm-playground/downloads/llm-playground-all.zip"
        download>download all the workshops</a
      >
      to edit the files and run them locally.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A few examples need an extra connection.</strong> Web search sends
      a query to an online search service. Semantic retrieval in RAG needs a separate
      embedding model, which the connection help explains how to set up. You can
      try RAG with keyword search using just your chat model.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <Connection />
  {
    lessons.map((lesson, index) => (
      <Lesson lesson={lesson} number={index + 1} />
    ))
  }
  <Fragment set:html={outcomesIntroHTML} />
  {outcomes.map((outcome) => <Outcome outcome={outcome} />)}
  <h2 id="shared-code">Shared model functions</h2>
  <p>
    You’ll notice that the examples call functions such as <code>ask</code> and
    <code>grounded</code> rather than writing out the whole model request each time.
    We’ve put that shared code in <code>shared.js</code> so the examples can focus
    on the part you’re experimenting with. If you want to follow a request further,
    open the file below to see how those helpers send messages, retrieve source material,
    and decide when a tool loop should stop.
  </p>
  <Disclosure title="Read shared.js"
    ><CodeSnippet wrap code={shared} language="js" title="shared.js" />
  </Disclosure>
  <Fragment set:html={resourcesHTML} />
</WeekPost>
<script is:inline type="module" src="/llm-playground/app.js"></script>
<script is:inline type="module" src="/llm-playground/visual-outcomes/app.js"
></script>
